Package: vellum
Type: Package
Title: Publication-Grade Scientific Figures with Executable Venue Specifications
Version: 0.5.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Vellum", "Authors", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           email = "techreyanda@gmail.com"),
    person("Loukas", "Theodosiou", role = c("ctb", "cph"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0001-6418-4652",
                       "Author of the ltc colour palettes bundled in R/palettes-ltc.R; see LICENSE.note")))
Description: A figure destined for a journal is a physical artefact with a
    published specification, and 'vellum' treats that specification as
    executable. It provides a provenance-tracked registry of journal artwork
    specifications, physical-unit converters so that no visual constant is a
    magic number, a design-token layer from which every theme is derived, a
    colour engine that constructs perceptually uniform ramps in CIE LCh with
    lightness linearisation and chroma-bisection gamut mapping, auditing of any
    palette under simulated colour-vision deficiency and greyscale using
    CIEDE2000, exact-physical-size composition and export, and a pre-flight
    compliance report that checks a plot against a venue before submission,
    and a provenance-bearing institutional and scientific-journal identity
    compiler with semantic roles, output modes, explicit typography fallbacks,
    accessible alternatives, extension, audit, and export. A role-bearing
    university extension compiles 50 provenance-aware institutional systems for
    figures, reports, presentations, dashboards, and future document templates
    without bundling protected marks or proprietary fonts.
    A scientific-illustration engine reads SVG artwork into real vector
    geometry rather than rasterising it, resolves stroke weights at the size the
    figure is actually drawn, and reports the licence position of every borrowed
    asset. A diagram grammar with typed relations builds participant-flow,
    causal, compartmental, pathway, pipeline and timeline figures that are
    checked for the errors those figures can actually contain.
